e problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$).\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=1$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$)." 13194139535941 true] [17592186375476 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375477 13194139535941 true] [17592186375476 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375478 13194139535941 true] [17592186375476 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375479 13194139535941 true] [17592186375476 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375480 13194139535941 true] [17592186375476 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375481 13194139535941 true] [17592186375476 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535941 true] [17592186375476 1041 "selection" 13194139535941 true] [17592186375476 1104 #edb/ref 17592186375478 13194139535941 true] [17592186375476 1110 #uuid "a0155c45-6d81-58c9-bea8-62c94584b455" 13194139535941 true] [17592186375477 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535941 true] [17592186375477 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535941 true] [17592186375477 1111 #uuid "48e1e1b6-c01c-54ea-ad81-438e60889d9d" 13194139535941 true] [17592186375478 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535941 true] [17592186375478 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535941 true] [17592186375478 1111 #uuid "afe1d8d7-01ea-5600-a003-6635599c427b" 13194139535941 true] [17592186375479 1034 "I only" 13194139535941 true] [17592186375479 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535941 true] [17592186375479 1111 #uuid "38292adc-a31d-55af-9402-4c78d96723cf" 13194139535941 true] [17592186375480 1034 "III only" 13194139535941 true] [17592186375480 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535941 true] [17592186375480 1111 #uuid "95baf817-6def-5390-a1c9-92ecce806127" 13194139535941 true] [17592186375481 1034 "II only" 13194139535941 true] [17592186375481 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535941 true] [17592186375481 1111 #uuid "e96f3505-6ce8-5fc3-aaff-e01728d9c51c" 13194139535941 true] [17592186375482 1017 #edb/ref 17592186375483 13194139535941 true] [17592186375482 1017 #edb/ref 17592186375486 13194139535941 true] [17592186375482 1017 #edb/ref 17592186375489 13194139535941 true] [17592186375482 1089 "q-338004" 13194139535941 true] [17592186375482 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535941 true] [17592186375482 1100 "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{″}-2x{y}^{′}+y=0$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}=x$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}-xy+4x=0$ is {{field-3}}." 13194139535941 true] [17592186375482 1113 #uuid "8da06f22-6a38-578e-b590-3e2d4a9025f6" 13194139535941 true] [17592186375482 1145 "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{″}-2x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}=x\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=x,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-xy+4x=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-xy=-4x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-4x,$ which is different from zero." 13194139535941 true] [17592186375483 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375484 13194139535941 true] [17592186375483 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375485 13194139535941 true] [17592186375483 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194